  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a persistent dry cough, which is a classic indicator of bronchial asthma, particularly cough-variant asthma. Palpitations are frequently reported in asthma cases due to transient hypoxia, physiological stress from breathing difficulties, or as a common side effect of beta-agonist inhalers used for symptom relief. The observation that infections are not resolving quickly points to underlying chronic airway inflammation and impaired mucociliary clearance, both of which delay recovery in asthmatic individuals. Intermittent throat pain that improves is likely secondary to mechanical irritation from chronic coughing, mouth breathing, or post-nasal drip. Conditions like pneumonia, common cold, or simple allergies fail to fully account for the chronicity, cardiovascular symptom (palpitations), and prolonged infection recovery. Thus, bronchial asthma best aligns with the clinical picture.",
  "final_answer": "bronchial asthma"
}

